          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Do bodies disagree about using a short-acting reliever on its own in asthma?",
          "answer": "The use of a short-acting reliever (SABA) alone in asthma is generally no longer considered appropriate at any severity. Current guidelines emphasize that all adults and adolescents with asthma should receive inhaled-corticosteroid-containing therapy. This is because SABA-only treatment does not address the underlying inflammation and is linked to severe asthma exacerbations, even in individuals with infrequent symptoms. Frequent SABA use (several canisters per year) is recognized as a sign of increased risk (guideline-asthma-diagnosis-and-stepwise-therapy).\n\nHowever, there is a divergence in guidance regarding the preferred reliever in mild asthma:\n\n*   **Global strategy guidance** recommends an inhaled-corticosteroid-and-formoterol combination as the preferred reliever across all steps of asthma management.\n*   **Some national panel guidance** indicates that a short-acting bronchodilator reliever, used alongside a regular controller, remains appropriate in mild disease (guideline-asthma-diagnosis-and-stepwise-therapy).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
